# Duane "Keffe D" Davis found guilty in 1996 murder of rapper Tupac Shakur
# 682 spoken words, 65 nodes
SAY   Duane "Keffe D" Davis found guilty in 1996 murder of rapper Tupac Shakur.
PAUSE 1200
SAY   By Anoushka Mutanda-Dougherty.
PAUSE 1200
SAY   Ex-gang boss found guilty in 1996 murder of rapper Tupac Shakur.
PAUSE 400
SAY   Former gang boss Duane "Keffe D" Davis has been found guilty of orchestrating the murder of Tupac Shakur - the first conviction in a cold case that has intrigued hip-hop fans for nearly 30 years.
PAUSE 500
SAY   The jury found Davis guilty of one count of murder with a deadly weapon for the rap superstar's death in a drive-by shooting in Las Vegas on 7 September 1996.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Clad in a black suit, the 63-year-old stood impassively in the Las Vegas court as the verdict was read, before saying he would appeal. Shakur's relatives held hands, some hugging and crying.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Shakur was 25 when he was slain at the height of his career as one of rap's most influential voices. He has sold more than 75 million records worldwide.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Prosecutors had argued during the trial that although Davis, a former leader of the South Side Compton Crips street gang, did not fire the gun that killed Shakur, he ordered the shooting and supplied the gun that his nephew Orlando Anderson used to open fire.
PAUSE 500
SAY   They told the court that Davis had planned the shooting as retaliation after Anderson was involved in a fight with Shakur and the rapper's crew in Las Vegas just hours earlier.
PAUSE 500
SAY   For 30 years, the case remained one of America's most famous cold cases, spurring countless conspiracies - but never a conviction, until now.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Shakur's murder happened at a time of intense rivalry between the East Coast and West Coast rap scene and amid gang feuds between the Bloods and Crips, which had deep roots within hip-hop at the time.
PAUSE 500
SAY   His family appeared emotional in court, his sister embracing a prosecutor after the verdict was read while others sat crying nearby.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Outside, devoted fans of Shakur were crying and cheering.
PAUSE 500
SAY   The key evidence in the case were Davis' own words, where he repeatedly and publicly acknowledged being inside the white Cadillac from where the deadly shots were fired.
PAUSE 500
SAY   He had told authorities about his role in Shakur's death during a secret 2008 police interview related to the death of fellow rapper Notorious B.I.G., real name Christopher Wallace.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Davis elaborated further on his role in his 2019 memoir, Compton Street Legend.
PAUSE 500
SAY   But his book and media interviews voided a protective agreement he had established with authorities that had blocked any criminal charges being filed, with the court ruling his police interview could be used in the murder trial against him.
PAUSE 500
SAY   In closing arguments, prosecutor Binu Palal told jurors to take Davis at his word and find him guilty.
PAUSE 500
SAY   For years, he argued, Davis had been telling people "that he is responsible for Tupac Shakur's murder" through documentaries, his memoir, podcasts and a police interview.
PAUSE 500
SAY   "He's the shot caller - in every instance in every iteration he's the leader of the South Side Crips," Palal told the jury.
PAUSE 500
SAY   "When you have the shot caller sitting shotgun when shots are fired, there's no doubt he's responsible."
PAUSE 500
SAY   Throughout the trial, Davis' defence team tried to persuade jurors that his comments were fiction - bravado and exaggeration intended to sell books and make money.
PAUSE 500
SAY   In closing arguments, his lawyer Michael Sanft urged the jury to discredit his client as a liar who just told tales to sell books.
PAUSE 500
SAY   "There's no proof," he told the jury.
PAUSE 500
SAY   But within hours of the jury being handed the case on Monday, they reached a guilty verdict.
PAUSE 500
SAY   Just before the court emptied out, Clark County District Court Judge Carli Kierny set Davis' sentencing for 13 October.
PAUSE 500
SAY   While reading out some procedural steps in the case, Davis appeared to interrupt by raising his hand.
PAUSE 500
SAY   "I would like my stuff," he told the judge, mentioning some personal items including his phone.
PAUSE 500
SAY   He continued: "I would like to appeal this matter."
PAUSE 500
SAY   The judge explained that this could happen after sentencing.
